  "image_name": "00e935adf71d8ad2c1e4eb1ecc1d0823.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a macrophage recognizes and engulfs an apoptotic cell through several receptor–ligand pathways centered on exposed phosphatidylserine (PtdSer) on the dying cell surface. The apoptotic cell is shown coated with purple surface molecules, and multiple macrophage receptors contact it either directly or through bridging proteins. Direct recognition appears to involve TIM4, BAI1, CD300b, and Stabilin 2. Other pathways use soluble opsonins: C1q/C3b/C4 connect to complement receptors CR1, CR3, and/or CR4, while MFGE8 links the apoptotic cell to αvβ3 or αvβ5 integrins. GAS6 or PROS1 seem to bridge PtdSer to the tyrosine kinase receptors MER or AXL.  \n\nThe diagram also includes inhibitory signaling: CD47 on the apoptotic cell interacts with SIRPα on the macrophage, representing a “don’t eat me” checkpoint that can oppose clearance. Overall, the image emphasizes that apoptotic cell uptake is not mediated by a single receptor, but by a network of partially overlapping recognition systems that combine direct binding, opsonin-dependent binding, and signaling receptors to drive efferocytosis.",
